# Agent Rules and Skills

## Ownership

- Keep standing project constraints in `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`. Agent entry points
  explain how to load those rules rather than duplicating their contents.
- Keep task recipes in skills. A standing constraint must remain discoverable
  without invoking a particular skill.
- Edit the file that owns a convention rather than creating a competing rule.

## Rule frontmatter

Each `.mdc` file starts with `description`, `globs`, and `alwaysApply` keys.
The description states what the rule covers and when it applies. Use
repository-relative, unquoted, comma-separated glob patterns; leave `globs:`
empty for rules selected by description alone.

Reserve always-on rules for context required on every task. Scope other rules
by file patterns or task descriptions. Keep each rule focused on one area and
approximately 150 lines or fewer.

## Content

- Ground constraints in confirmed project facts, with paths and symbols pointing
  to canonical examples. Separate proposed conventions from established ones.
- Link to canonical documentation for deep context instead of copying it.
- Describe concrete requirements and their reasons rather than vague advice.
- Keep credentials, personal data, and machine-specific absolute paths out of
  shared instructions. Use placeholders for sensitive examples.
- Avoid restating general language or tool documentation unless the project
  makes a specific choice that agents need to follow.

## Maintenance

When an underlying fact changes, update its owning rule in the same change.
Check sibling rules and entry points for contradictions and broken references.
Before recording a user correction as a permanent rule, confirm that it is
intended to apply beyond the current task.

If an existing review-agent configuration mirrors these rules, update affected
review constraints in the same change. Preserve one canonical source; mirror
constraints only where that review integration requires a separate format.

## Skills

A `SKILL.md` starts with `name` and `description` frontmatter. Its description
states when to invoke it. The body supplies the task procedure, necessary
references, and verifiable completion criteria, while relying on shared rules
for standing project constraints.
